## Defensive Tactics

Here are some examples:

A. Train and certify all employees to use a common <strong>task tracking</strong> system responding to alerts (Vendor: Jira).

B. <strong>Escalate</strong> tasks which are not resolved in a timely way (Vendor: PagerDuty)

C. Train and certify all email holders to recognize <strong>phishing</strong> attacks (Vendor: Knowbe4).

D. Get customers and vendors to <strong>secure emails</strong> using SPF etc in DNS (Vendor: Virtru).

E. Use <strong>MFA</strong> (Multi-Factor Authentication) for all logins (vendor: Microsoft Entra ID P2).

F. Provide each employee with a <strong>password manager</strong> to make it convenient to generate strong passwords and autofill websites so password reuse is not necessary.

G. Limit <strong>permission to backup</strong> media to separate accounts so they can't be deleted and encrypted for ransomware.

H. Assign <strong>least privilege</strong> permissions to limit each account to the minimum permissions needed to do their job.

I. <strong>Segment</strong> the network into zones, with a firewall between each zone, and only allow the minimum ports and protocols needed for each zone. This limits lateral movement by attackers.

J. Configure each device based on security recommendations in its <a target="_blank" href="https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks/"><strong>CIS Benchmark</strong></a>.

K. <strong>Automate installs</strong> of the latest version of every component (OS, utilities, apps) on laptops and servers in case of infection. This include drivers, firmware, TLS version, etc.

L. Verify that each device is running the <strong>latest version</strong> of patches of OS, utilities, apps.

M. After each install, create a hash of each static (executable) file, then generate them repeatedly on a schedule to see if they have been changed to a potentially hacked version. <strong>File integrity</strong> host-based IDPS (Intrusion Detection and Protection System) Vendor: <a target="_blank" href="https://www.ossec.net/">Atomic OSSEC</a>, Solarwinds, <a target="_blank" href="https://www.tripwire.com/state-of-security/ot-security-risks-challenges-and-securing-your-environment">Tripwire</a> <a target="_blank" href="https://github.com/Tripwire/tripwire-open-source" title="WARNING: from 2018">open source</a>)

N. Perform regular <strong>data backups</strong> to cloud for enabling data recovery.

O. <strong>Verify recovery</strong> to prove the ability to recover from backups and reinstalls.

P. Send <strong>alerts to SIEM</strong> (<a target="_blank" href="https://wilsonmar.github.io/siem/">Security Information and Event Management</strong> system) upon irregular logins, when a device is added to or removed from the network, when data streams are larger or smaller than normal, etc. (Vendor: Microsoft Defender, Prometheus Cloud)

Q. Regularly schedule a fake test attack sent to each device, to ensure that <strong>alerts works</strong> in SIEM.

R. Create a <strong>SBOM</strong> (Software Bill of Materials) of each open-source library package in the chain of references. Flag suspicious version changes, then scan the code of each package for vulnerabilities such as exfiltrating data externally.

S. Detect <strong>anomalies in network</strong> traffic, such as a sudden increase in the number of connections to a server, or a sudden increase in the number of bytes sent to a server.

T. Train and certify each employee to manage action levels from <strong>trends in dashboards</strong> (Vendor: Grafana)


### API & OT (Operational Technology) Security

U. Configure every node with certificates for two-way encryption using <strong>mTLS</strong> (Mutual TLS) between every node for all network connections.

V. Have API servers <a target="_blank" href="https://portswigger.net/web-security/jwt#exploiting-flawed-jwt-signature-verification">verify</a> JWT (JSON Web Tokens) signatures to block attempts at user impersonation. Sign JWT tokens with a private key, then verify the signature with a public key.

W. Enforce a strict whitelist of permitted hosts for the jku header.

X. Always set an expiration date for any tokens issued.

Y. Avoid sending tokens in URL parameter string, which end up in logs.

Z. Include the aud (audience) claim (or similar) to specify the intended recipient of the token. This prevents it from being used on different websites.

AA. Enable the issuing server to revoke tokens (on logout, for example).

AB. Setup secure video surveillance of remote hardware devices to provide a history of accesses.

AC. Use API management servers to assign API tokens so that the number of API calls per minute can be tracked and limited.

AD. Setup SNMP pings with authentication 


Standards for ICS (Industrial Control Systems) are defined by NIST (National Institute of Standards and Technology) as ISA/IEC-62443 or ISA-99 in the US and IEC (International Electrotechnical Commission) in Europe.
